WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:08.000
NyayaSiddhi version zero point two is an open Indian element-by-element development benchmark, with data and a scorer.

2
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:14.711
It is a development benchmark: descriptive, with no ranking. This film is for people who want to take part.

3
00:00:14.711 --> 00:00:22.550
It covers what the benchmark is, why it was needed, how to take part and submit, and how the leaderboard is built and scored.

4
00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:32.393
The task is simple to state. Given the allegation-side facts of a court matter and a named offence contract, a system returns PROOF or NOT_PROOF.

5
00:00:32.393 --> 00:00:36.511
PROOF means every required element of the named contract is established.

6
00:00:36.511 --> 00:00:43.832
The items are real Indian High Court orders on three offence families: criminal breach of trust, cheating, and cheque dishonour.

7
00:00:43.832 --> 00:00:51.725
The label is the court's own outcome, as extracted from judgments by models and filtered by extractor agreement. It is not human-verified.

8
00:00:53.300 --> 00:01:01.526
There are two released sets. The first, obj1_dev, has one hundred seventy-seven items, of which one hundred seventy-one are scored.

9
00:01:01.526 --> 00:01:11.384
We have seen it, and used it to design fixes. The second, obj1b_minus_cruelty, has one hundred fifty-four items, of which one hundred forty-three are scored.

10
00:01:11.384 --> 00:01:20.175
It is exposed: a model we use has read it. Both are public. This is a development benchmark, with no hidden split in version zero point two.

11
00:01:20.175 --> 00:01:22.875
It is descriptive, and there is no ranking.

12
00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:28.586
Why was it needed? Here is the reason, as the dataset card prints it.

13
00:01:28.586 --> 00:01:34.713
We found no public board that scores whether a stated claim is proved from facts, element by element.

14
00:01:34.713 --> 00:01:41.386
This release lets outside models, harnesses and our own systems be compared on the same items with one scorer.

15
00:01:41.386 --> 00:01:45.450
It is a comparison place, not a claim that any system proves cases.

16
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:55.387
From their abstracts and card pages, these are works we looked at. LegalBench: legal reasoning tasks written by legal professionals.

17
00:01:55.387 --> 00:02:02.566
LexGLUE: English legal language understanding tasks. IL-TUR: Indian legal text understanding and reasoning tasks.

18
00:02:02.566 --> 00:02:09.238
ILDC: Indian Supreme Court cases annotated with court decisions, for judgment prediction and explanation.

19
00:02:09.238 --> 00:02:16.226
BhashaBench-Legal: Indian legal knowledge questions from judicial service and bar exams. Are LLMs Court-Ready?

20
00:02:16.226 --> 00:02:22.199
: India's public legal exams as a proxy for legal competence, with lawyer-graded long answers.

21
00:02:22.199 --> 00:02:27.599
And JUREX-4E: an expert-annotated knowledge base of the elements of criminal charges.

22
00:02:27.599 --> 00:02:35.160
The works we looked at test legal knowledge from exams, legal text understanding, case outcome prediction or retrieval.

23
00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:42.975
NyayaSiddhi scores whether a system says an offence is made out, on items the court found made out and on items it did not.

24
00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:54.338
Every result is two numbers, shown together. Wrongly supported is counted over the items the court did not establish: how many the system still marked as supported.

25
00:02:54.338 --> 00:03:00.217
Coverage is counted over the items the court established: how many the system marked as supported.

26
00:03:00.217 --> 00:03:08.975
A system that refuses every item shows zero wrongly supported and zero coverage by construction. So zero wrongly supported is not read on its own.

27
00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:20.353
To take part, you prepare two files. A prediction file has one row per item: the item id and a verdict, PROOF or NOT_PROOF, with every item exactly once.

28
00:03:20.353 --> 00:03:23.766
A missing item is an error, never a silent NOT_PROOF.

29
00:03:23.766 --> 00:03:34.392
A manifest names your system and version, pins every model id with a revision, and gives the run date, the scorer sha, the split, the unit, and your publish consent.

30
00:03:34.392 --> 00:03:45.275
We do not ask for weights, prompts or code. Before you submit, check it yourself: score_entry.py runs the organisers' check and the released scorer on your own machine.

31
00:03:45.275 --> 00:03:53.325
Nothing is uploaded. There is no key, no account and no model call. The output is a self-scored preview, not a published row.

32
00:03:54.900 --> 00:04:02.993
You submit by a Hugging Face pull request on the dataset page. publish_consent is false for a private local score with score_entry.py.

33
00:04:02.993 --> 00:04:07.549
It must be true before you open a pull request, and the checks refuse false.

34
00:04:07.549 --> 00:04:14.802
True only says you would allow your row, your manifest and your predictions file to be published. A merge publishes them.

35
00:04:14.802 --> 00:04:19.897
A file whose verdicts equal the released labels on every scored item is not accepted.

36
00:04:19.897 --> 00:04:25.293
Training on public items and any overlap check are declared in the manifest, not verified.

37
00:04:25.293 --> 00:04:30.508
Rows are append-only: a correction is a new row, and a withdrawal leaves a visible row.

38
00:04:30.508 --> 00:04:39.200
As of 21 October 2026, the submission path has been exercised only on a synthetic file from a clean checkout; no outside party has submitted yet.

39
00:04:40.700 --> 00:04:50.792
Here is the leaderboard as it is built. Each row shows wrongly supported and coverage as k of n, with exact ninety-five percent intervals, by offence group and pooled.

40
00:04:50.792 --> 00:05:00.702
It also shows the unit, the stack, the run date, the status and the contamination flag. Statuses are self-reported, re-scored, reproduced, and third-party verified.

41
00:05:00.702 --> 00:05:08.800
Rows are listed in a fixed order, by split and system name, never by a figure. This is the app's Benchmarks page. There is no ranking.

42
00:05:10.300 --> 00:05:12.680
Scoring is the same for every submission.

43
00:05:12.680 --> 00:05:23.300
The organisers record the pull request head sha, run the checker, which must print PASS, score with the released scorer, build and validate the row, and re-read the head before merge.

44
00:05:23.300 --> 00:05:30.845
The scorer is open: it uses only the Python standard library, and it is pinned in SHA256SUMS. Intervals are exact Clopper-Pearson.

45
00:05:30.845 --> 00:05:39.376
Wrongly supported has an exact one-sided ninety-five percent upper bound and an exact two-sided interval. Coverage has an exact two-sided interval.

46
00:05:39.376 --> 00:05:48.894
Errored items are counted both ways: not as a proof, and as a proof, the worst case. When two systems are compared, a direction is stated only if both counts agree.

47
00:05:48.894 --> 00:05:56.439
A family with fewer than ten items in either label is descriptive. The rules fail closed. There is no rank and no composite score.

48
00:05:56.439 --> 00:06:04.100
The contamination flag is set by the organisers, never the submitter, and a row stays self-reported until a clean-checkout re-score.

49
00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:09.338
We built this benchmark, and we enter our own systems in it.

50
00:06:09.338 --> 00:06:18.993
Our rows are marked, they sit in the same table, they are checked by the same script and they carry the same intervals. Another team member re-scores them.

51
00:06:18.993 --> 00:06:23.229
That is an internal consistency check, not independent verification.

52
00:06:23.229 --> 00:06:32.697
Here are our own system's figures, with their limits on screen: self-reported, an organiser entry, on the dev stack, on sets we have both seen, one run.

53
00:06:32.697 --> 00:06:38.926
On obj1b minus cruelty: none of one hundred twenty-eight false proofs, with none of fifteen covered.

54
00:06:38.926 --> 00:06:47.710
On obj1 dev: none of sixty-four with errors as not proof, but one of sixty-four with errors as proof, with none of one hundred seven covered.

55
00:06:47.710 --> 00:06:50.575
Zero wrongly supported is not read on its own.

56
00:06:52.100 --> 00:06:58.483
Two things do not exist yet. There is no hidden split in version zero point two. The mechanism for one is planned.

57
00:06:58.483 --> 00:07:02.514
The organisers would hold it, and there is no independent custodian yet.

58
00:07:02.514 --> 00:07:10.241
A hash commitment to a hidden split is published when the strict court-established holdout reaches thirty or more, and we promise no date.

59
00:07:10.241 --> 00:07:17.800
Today it is three court-established and ten court-rejected, real, model-extracted. And hosted evaluation is planned. It does not exist.

60
00:07:19.300 --> 00:07:30.925
NyayaSiddhi is a place to compare, with no ranking. The NyayaSiddhi dataset is on Hugging Face, under AxisMeru slash nyayasiddhi, and the app has a Benchmarks page.
